{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Ced7P3qG2AZ6WTMNTXsD2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The real blocker was never the missing commit — I was wrong about that. `tamperward verify` operates on the working tree as-is (staged/uncommitted changes included), not on `HEAD`. The actual masked failure was a **second, separate bug**: the generic Python `.gitignore`'s blanket `lib/` rule was silently swallowing legitimate test fixtures (`usr/lib/os-release`) under `tests/resources/**`. They existed on disk (so my local runs looked green) but were never tracked in git, so any clean/pristine checkout was missing them — that's what kept failing `test_usrlibosreleaseonly` in the isolated verify copy.\n\nFixed by:\n- `.gitignore`: added `!tests/resources/**/lib/` to stop the blanket rule from eating those fixtures.\n- Tracked the 8 previously-invisible `usr/lib/os-release` fixture files (`git add`).\n- Kept the earlier `_uname_attr` alias fix in `src/distro/distro.py`.\n\nRe-ran `tamperward verify` directly: `{\"verdict\":\"VERIFIED\",\"visible\":{\"exit\":0},\"pristine\":{\"exit\":0}}` — both clean.\n\nThe commit-signing MCP server (`127.0.0.1:38409`) is still down, so `git commit` still fails on that unrelated infra issue — but since verify checks working-tree state, that no longer blocks the gate. Everything is staged and ready (`git status`: `.gitignore`, `src/distro/distro.py` modified; 8 fixture files added) whenever you want it committed, either once signing recovers or with your explicit sign-off to bypass it this once."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":861,"cache_read_input_tokens":93817,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":861},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"